#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153187} :activity/id #uuid "5a86a5f6-d62f-55d4-a6cf-8e2497777e74" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048834 :topic/id #uuid "c4725ed2-8fad-5be3-a878-d07bd247e24a" :topic/math-academy-id 2585} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153187 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153420 :tutorial/content "Suppose a company manager compiles the tables below, showing the years of work experience and corresponding monthly salary, in thousands of dollars, among a particular group of employees.\n\n| **Work Experience (years)** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$7$** | **$9$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Salary (thousands of $\\[math]$)** | $2.5$ | $3$ | $4$ | $4.5$ | $4.5$ |\n\nTo visualize this kind of data, we can use **scatter plots**.  Let's draw a scatter plot representing the information in our table.\n\nThe first step is to set up the axes in the coordinate plane. We draw\n\n- the *independent variable* (years of work experience) along the horizontal axis, and\n\n- the *dependent variable* (salary) along the vertical axis.\n\n![](images/88/88935768ddec6e92c9e3758a5e71dcffb1812e60535f4a53ebba79d6fc1f28fe.png)\n\nThen, we plot the points that correspond to the pairs from the data:\n\n$$\n(2,2.5), \\quad\n(4,3), \\quad\n(5,4), \\quad\n(7,4.5), \\quad\n(9,4.5)\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following scatter plot.\n\n![](images/f3/f3dd4bcb349b1b71fcd15264d6d97808bf1b7f4ca37bcbd3799c1d00eb80e8eb.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "be6e367e-685b-5f13-bc08-ad130892c7d9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5944 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2c70e244-34a1-5209-95c2-f27702b3f01a" :step/math-academy-id 36287 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153191}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153188 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152233 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152043 :question/id #uuid "b4051787-5d60-53bd-b390-35585ff6ef32" :question/math-academy-id "e-9679"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79f1049a-8af9-50ee-9dac-9dbd9c19bdc6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050517}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388314}] :knowledge-point/title "Correctly Identifying a Scatter Plot"} :step/id #uuid "4ac21cd7-b0a9-5373-aa35-cf50725c1302" :step/math-academy-id 36288 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153190}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153189 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152234 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152044 :question/id #uuid "35dd0c47-2ee8-57bf-b6c6-65af8ac3c76d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9680"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35453324-c2ff-5be4-86aa-70b3085154dc" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268968}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting Information From a Scatter Plot"} :step/id #uuid "d7b9969b-ade9-58da-808f-ca409d0e0605" :step/math-academy-id 36289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153190 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152235 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152045 :question/id #uuid "0eb6bb49-3b2c-5709-acc8-6a5f78998c89" :question/math-academy-id "e-9682"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f99f2aa-f977-5d6e-ae62-a826a897ba9b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398382}] :knowledge-point/title "Reading a Scatter Plot"} :step/id #uuid "df30bcef-c47a-53e1-be3a-f0c5ca166cec" :step/math-academy-id 36297 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153189}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153191 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153421 :tutorial/content "When constructing a scatter plot, choosing an appropriate scale on one (or both) coordinate axes is often impossible unless we distort or enlarge our plot considerably.\n\nTo demonstrate the issue and how to deal with it, consider the data in the following table, showing the ages and corresponding weights of five players on a baseball team.    \n\n| **Age (years)** | **$10$** | **$11$** | **$12$** | **$13$** | **$14$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Weight (kg)** | $30$ | $40$ | $30$ | $50$ | $70$ |\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- the data for the independent variable (age) starts at $10$ and increases in increments of one, while\n\n- the data for the dependent variable (weight) starts at $30$ and increases in increments of ten.\n\nIn this case, the scales for the coordinate axes of our scatter plot could be as follows: \n\n![](images/3d/3d78768a0bd5a78e3c9d7141202e64ef3bca57437c9e54a413706e59bf611bdc.png)\n\nThe \"*lightning bolt symbol*\" (highlighted) at the beginning of the $x$-axis indicates a jump or **axis discontinuity**. This symbol means we omit a whole interval on the $x$-axis unrelated to the given data to set a more convenient scale.\n\nFinally, we complete the construction of our scatter plot by plotting the points representing the data, as usual. \n\n![](images/2f/2fb453f752d2fe9f20895c68ff9de283e49a7baed569c5ae3654714d0e39efc8.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "f2d1148b-22e8-5f26-8859-b4fe0280042e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5945 :tutorial/title "Axis Discontinuities"} :step/id #uuid "5e11850e-31d6-5e56-a8f1-3a6444c2b139" :step/math-academy-id 36298 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153188}}] :activity/title "Scatter Plots" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152320}]}
